  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Elton John Aids Foundation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Every year, over 110,000 babies are born to HIV-positive mothers in Tanzania. Without intervention, nearly half of these children will die before their second birthday. Many HIV infections in infants can be prevented if HIV-positive pregnant women and their infants receive all of the recommended drugs at the right time. Tanzania has had a national programme for Prevention of Mother-to-Child Transmission of HIV (PMTCT) since 2000. Though most HIV-positive women go through some of the processes to prevent HIV transmission, many positive women and their infants do not complete the full course of recommended drugs and interventions. This project aims to reduce the number of infants born with HIV by strengthening the demand for health services by pregnant women and testing an alternative PMTCT drug regimen (preliminary studies show it to be more effective than the current regimen used). It will work in two of Dar-es-Salaam&apos;s three municipalities. Health workers based in communities will encourage all pregnant women to learn their HIV status, attend antenatal care four times, and deliver their babies in health facilities. Women who fall out of the system will be followed up. This will improve maternal and child outcomes for all women, but importantly it will increase the likelihood that HIV-positive pregnant women and their infants get the full course of drugs to prevent HIV transmission. This will result in fewer infants infected with HIV. The project will compare the new drug regimen with the current regimen and provide evidence to influence Tanzania&apos;s national PMTCT policy and protocols.</narrative>
  </description>
